  "strategy": "### Approach and rationale\n\nWe propose a genetic algorithm (GA) approach to address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by evolving a population of candidate functions to minimize C\u2085. GA is well-suited for exploring complex landscapes with multiple local optima, making it a promising alternative to gradient-based methods.\n\n### Mathematical specification\n\n- **Variables**: h[i] \u2208 [0,1], i=0,\u2026,n_points-1\n- **Objective**: Minimize max_k (h \u2a2f (1-h))[k] * (2/n_points)\n- **Constraints**:\n  1. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n  2. \u2211h[i] * (2/n_points) = 1 \u21d2 \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2\n- **Operations**:\n  1. Generate initial population\n  2. Evaluate fitness\n  3. Selection via tournament\n  4. Crossover between pairs\n  5. Mutation of individuals\n  6. Replacement with best offspring\n\n### Implementation procedure\n\n1. **Initialization**:\n   - Set n_points=140, required_sum=70\n   - Initialize population of size 200\n   - If initial_h_values exists, use its scaled version as one member\n   - Generate remaining population by random sampling within [0,1], scaled to sum=70\n   \n2. **Fitness evaluation**:\n   - For each candidate h in population:\n      - Compute overlap integral using FFT-based convolution\n      - Record C\u2085 value as fitness score\n\n3. **Selection**:\n   - Perform tournament selection (k=10)\n   - Select top 50% of population for reproduction\n\n4. **Crossover**:\n   - Use single-point crossover on selected parents\n   - For each pair, randomly choose a point and swap segments\n    \n5. **Mutation**:\n   - Apply Gaussian mutation with sigma=0.1 to 50% of offspring\n   - Clip to [0,1] and rescale sum to 70\n    \n6. **Replacement**:\n   - Replace bottom 50% of population with new offspring\n   - Keep best candidate at each generation\n\n7. **Termination**:\n   - Stop when time budget exhausted or no improvement for 100 generations\n\n### Search choices and useful variations\n\n- **Population size**: 200\n- **Tournament size**: 10\n- **Crossover probability**: 0.8\n- **Mutation probability**: 0.2\n- **Elitism**: Keep top 5% of population\n- **Replacement strategy**: Keep all best candidates\n\n### Compute budget and robustness\n\n- Fitness evaluation: 0.05s per individual\n- Mutation and crossover: 0.01s each\n- Expected generations: 1000 within 1100s\n- Fallback: Use initial_h_values if no improvement\n\n### Validation and return contract\n\n- Ensure h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h)=70\n- Recompute C\u2085 via evaluator\n- Return best_h, best_val, n_points",
